# Research and baseline provenance

## Repository snapshot

The inspected `origin/main` snapshot is commit
[`ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc`](https://github.com/1337isnot1337/e-99/commit/ab6dd3336f9f8d440f2bada76edb32153391cddc),
with Git tree `bd5b3835d57cad67d387c7bb92687e88bf48f982` and theorem state
`2026-10-10.audit.234`. [`BASELINE.md`](BASELINE.md) records the manifest and source
hashes, validation commands, environment, and current results. A hash identifies bytes;
it does not attest that a theorem or citation is correct.

## Authority and claim lineage

Use the repository's existing authority order:

1. [`../STATE.md`](../STATE.md) and [`../state/theorem-state.yaml`](../state/theorem-state.yaml)
   determine lifecycle and current status.
2. The active target route maps and named theorem modules determine the current
   mathematical endpoint.
3. A report is a premise only when the exact claim is promoted in the ledger or route.
4. Unpromoted reports, merged branches, historical prompts, and archive files preserve
   research provenance; they do not independently establish a theorem.

The machine-readable entries retain stable claim IDs, target, exact source report or
module, audit/promotion origin, status, explicit theorem-ID dependencies, and registered
certificates where applicable. The artifact index adds lifecycle metadata to research
artifacts for navigation. Its generated status and graph files are views of the ledger,
not competing sources of truth.

## What the validators establish

The state checker validates the manifest against its schema, resolves registered
theorem-ID edges, rejects cycles in `depends_on`, applies the configured dependency
status rules, checks registered certificate paths, and compares generated indexes and
views. The certificate runner executes each registered script. Unit tests exercise
these tools and selected research scripts exercise their documented finite claims.

These checks do not independently reconstruct proofs, verify every intermediate lemma
inside an active module, validate external sources or redistribution rights, or constitute
external peer review. In particular, the theorem graph covers only dependencies recorded
between manifest IDs. The recorded IDs in this snapshot are unevenly distributed across
targets; Target A's active theorem stack is route/module-based and has no standalone
manifest entries. A certificate passes only the finite computation stated in its own
source. See [`BASELINE.md`](BASELINE.md) for the exact outputs and hashes.

## Attribution and historical material

The ledger's report paths, audit records, promotion origins, and Git history are the
available provenance trail for research claims. Preserve those stable paths and exact
scope labels when preparing a release. A commit author, branch name, merge event, or
contributor's AI-assistance disclosure is not by itself mathematical authorship or proof
credit. A separate publication audit must decide whether any content, source, or binary
may be redistributed and whether contributor names or private information may be shown.

No release tag was created for this baseline. The proposed future annotated-tag format
and its limits are recorded in [`BASELINE.md`](BASELINE.md); using it requires owner
authorization.

## Restored evidence identity

The [restoration register](restored-evidence.json) identifies 17 recovered artifacts,
their frozen source commits and hashes, and each restored body's SHA-256. All 12
reports retain unreviewed headers; five are executable finite verifiers. The report
headers and the one recorded whitespace normalization are administrative metadata.
Recovery, matching hashes and successful script execution do not supply proof review.
Canonical audit records control the exact statements they accept from these sources.

`python3 tools/check_restored_evidence.py` checks retained bodies without private Git
history and runs in ordinary prelaunch verification. In the complete private archive,
add `--source-root .` to verify the frozen Git objects and replay the documented
normalization. The source-object mode cannot run in a clean-history export. Changing
a restored argument requires an explicit provenance update and the applicable review;
regenerating its lifecycle header alone does not change its body identity.
